Why this exists. This slice published 4.5x to 20x for "check" against "raw" on the
string path and called it the largest single effect it measures. It is a figure about
THIS SLICE'S VALIDATOR, not about the policy decision 3 settles: 38 ns to validate a
36-byte ASCII string is about 1 ns per byte, and a decision should not be priced
against the slowest reasonable implementation of the thing being decided.

================================================================================
PART 1: correctness, which is the whole job
================================================================================

Byte identity cannot see a validator defect. A manifest is made of things that
ENCODE, so it carries no malformed input at all, and a fast validator that accepts a
surrogate would pass every gate this slice has. So the check is an exhaustive
differential test against an oracle written to be obviously right -- RFC 3629 table
3-7, one range per line -- and the oracle is not one of the implementations under
test, so agreement is evidence rather than a tautology.

