The published row was 4.47-4.54 on ascii, 15.08-15.28 on latin1, 19.87-20.05 on wide.
Two things changed and both are corrections rather than tuning:

  * C20. The set was SIX fields and should have been five. `ResultRaw.opaque_id` is a
    `bytes` field: proto3 puts no UTF-8 requirement on it, the generated codec reaches
    it through ak_tc_bytes and decode_str_raw, and no validator ever sees it. In the
    ascii set its 1,000 values are arbitrary bytes that the check arm REJECTED on the
    first bad byte, so it did less work than a validation and the ascii row understated
    the cost. Found by src/utf8check.cpp asserting that every string it validates is
    valid, which the table itself never did. One definition now, in harness.h, so the
    table and the validator gate cannot drift apart again.
  * The validator. The codec now calls the lead-byte table.

THE HEADLINE: on ascii -- which is what the manifest pins and what a real payload
mostly is -- the decode-side UTF-8 check costs 2.27x a raw copy, not 4.4x, and
+8.1 ns per string rather than +21.7.

AND: the core's validator is cheaper than the INCUMBENT'S OWN on all three sets.
2.27 against 2.56 on ascii, 15.9 against 19.6 on latin1, 19.6 against 34.4 on wide.
R14 says the comparison is against what production runs, and on the decode side of a
`string` field what production runs is protobuf's IsStructurallyValidUTF8. So
decision 3's check is not a cost the core imposes on a host that did not have one --
it is cheaper than the check the host is already paying for.

Read the latin1 and wide multipliers with their denominator in view: `raw` is 6 to 7
ns for a whole string, so a 16x is +105 ns and not a catastrophe of scale. The ratio
is large because copying 60 bytes is nearly free, not because validating them is slow.

================================================================================
PART 3: why the table beats the DFA, which was the first thing tried
================================================================================

The DFA is the textbook answer: 9 states, 12 byte classes, one table lookup and one
add per byte, no data-dependent branch. It measured 2.25x on ascii and WORSE THAN THE
SCALAR VERSION on wide (0.78x). The state is a serial dependency -- each byte's lookup
address depends on the previous byte's result -- and on uniform non-ASCII content the
branches it removes were being predicted correctly anyway, so it traded free branches
for a dependent load chain.

The lead-byte table keeps the scalar version's shape, consuming a whole sequence per
iteration, and removes what is actually wasted in it: the code-point arithmetic.
Validation never needs the value, only the ranges, and every range constraint in UTF-8
is a function of the lead byte alone -- overlong forms, surrogates and everything above
U+10FFFF are each exactly a constraint on the SECOND byte given the first. One packed
u32 per lead byte carries the length and that range, so it is one load per code point.
Three parallel byte arrays were three loads and measured 1.07x on latin1 against 1.05x
to 1.08x packed.

Both are kept and both are in the differential test. The DFA is not dead code: it is
the evidence for the sentence above, and a slice that deletes the thing it rejected
leaves the next reader to re-derive it.

WHAT IS NOT MEASURED

  * The effect on a WHOLE-PAYLOAD decode ratio. It is below this slice's across-build
    resolution: the string-path delta is 13.6 ns/string on ascii, P1.2 has 5 strings
    per element against a 384 ns/element decode, so the arithmetic says about 18% of
    the ffi decode -- which is 0.10 on a ratio of 0.55, inside R4's 0.240 bar. Two
    binaries cannot resolve it and one process cannot hold both, because the validator
    is a build-time choice in the generated binding. The string-path number is the
    measurement; the whole-payload number is arithmetic and is labelled as such. This
    is the same reasoning that withdrew the earlier "22 to 28 percent of a decode".
  * A SIMD validator. utf8_range is not in this tree -- STATE.md said it was and that
    was wrong, it was in a scratch directory from the upb arm that does not survive.
    protobuf's own validator is the ceiling instead, which is a better one for R14's
    purposes and costs nothing to have. What a real SIMD validator would add on top is
    open; the honest expectation is another 3x to 5x on ascii.
  * The encode side, and this is a correction to what I first wrote here. `ffi-valtc`
    reaches `ak_tc_utf8()` -- the CORE's validating transcoder, written in Rust in
    ffi/poc/codec/crates/ak-rt -- so nothing in this work unit touched it and its column
    has not moved. Only the DECODE path calls ak::utf8_valid, through `s_of` in the
    generated binding. Whether the core's own encode-side validator has the same 2x
    available to it is an open question and it is not a slice's to answer: R0 makes a
    change to existing behaviour in the shared core the aggregating session's.

